    \   First, we choose 2 dice out of 6 to show a 1. This can be done in $\binom{6}{2} = \frac{6!}{2!4!} = \frac{6 \times 5}{2 \times 1} = 15$ ways.
    \   Next, we choose 2 dice out of the remaining 4 dice to show a 2. This can be done in $\binom{4}{2} = \frac{4!}{2!2!} = \frac{4 \times 3}{2 \times 1} = 6$ ways.
