\begin{enumerate}
    \item The projection of a vector $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis is the component of $\mathbf{v}$ that lies along the $y$-axis. In other words, it is the scalar multiple of the unit vector in the $y$-direction, $\mathbf{j}$.
    \item Let $\mathbf{v} = \begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix}$. The projection of $\mathbf{v}$ onto the $y$-axis is given by
    $$ \text{proj}_{\mathbf{j}} \mathbf{v} = \frac{\mathbf{v} \cdot \mathbf{j}}{\|\mathbf{j}\|^2} \mathbf{j} = \frac{\begin{pmatrix} a \\ b \end{pmatrix} \cdot \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}}{\left\| \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} \right\|^2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \frac{b}{1^2} \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = b \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ b \end{pmatrix}. $$
    \item The matrix corresponding to this projection is the matrix whose columns are the projections of the standard basis vectors onto the $y$-axis. The projection of $\mathbf{i} = \begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}$ onto the $y$-axis is $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$, and the projection of $\mathbf{j} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$ onto the $y$-axis is $\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}$. Therefore, the projection matrix is
    $$ P = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 \end{pmatrix}. $$
\end{enumerate}
