\begin{enumerate}
    \item Use synthetic division to divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$.
    \item Find the remainder.
\end{enumerate}

First, we factor the divisor $x^2-3x+2 = (x-1)(x-2)$. 

We will divide $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $(x-1)(x-2)$. 
Using synthetic division with $x-1$, we have:

\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
1 & 1 & -3 & 4 & 11 & -9 \\
  &   & 1  & -2 & 2   & 13  \\ \hline
  & 1 & -2 & 2 & 13 & 4 
\end{array}
The quotient is $x^3-2x^2+2x+13$ and the remainder is 4.

Now we divide the remainder 4 by $x-2$. 
Using synthetic division with $x-2$, we have:

\begin{array}{c|cccc}
2 & 1 & -2 & 2 & 13 \\
  &   & 2  & 0 & 4  \\ \hline
  & 1 & 0 & 2 & 17 
\end{array}
The quotient is $x^2+2$ and the remainder is 17.

However, this is not correct. Let's try dividing $x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9$ by $x^2-3x+2$ directly.
We have
$$\begin{array}{r} x^2+1 \\ x^2-3x+2 \hspace{0.1in} \overline{\big) \hspace{0.1in} x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9} \\ \underline{-(x^4-3x^3+2x^2)} \\ 2x^2+11x-9 \\ \underline{-(2x^2-6x+4)} \\ 17x-13 \end{array}$$
The quotient is $x^2+1$ and the remainder is $17x-13$.

We can rewrite the given polynomial as: 
$x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = (x^2-3x+2)(x^2+Ax+B) + Cx+D$
Expanding the right side:
$x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = x^4 + Ax^3 + Bx^2 - 3x^3 - 3Ax^2 - 3Bx + 2x^2 + 2Ax + 2B + Cx+D$
$x^4-3x^3+4x^2+11x-9 = x^4 + (A-3)x^3 + (B-3A+2)x^2 + (2A-3B+C)x + (2B+D)$
Comparing coefficients:
$A-3 = -3 \implies A=0$
$B-3A+2 = 4 \implies B - 3(0) + 2 = 4 \implies B = 2$
$2A-3B+C = 11 \implies 2(0) - 3(2) + C = 11 \implies -6 + C = 11 \implies C = 17$
$2B+D = -9 \implies 2(2) + D = -9 \implies 4 + D = -9 \implies D = -13$
Therefore, the remainder is $17x-13$.

