\end{align*} 

We want to find the number of functions that are both even and odd.
A function that is even must satisfy $f(-x) = f(x)$.
A function that is odd must satisfy $f(-x) = -f(x)$.
For a function to be both even and odd, we must have $f(-x) = f(x)$ and $f(-x) = -f(x)$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
Therefore, we must have $f(x) = -f(x)$, which implies $2f(x) = 0$, so $f(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
Thus, the only function that is both even and odd is the function $f(x) = 0$ for all $x \in \mathbb{R}$.
Therefore, there is only 1 such function.

\end{align*}
